// Loop-header expressions with class machinery — two defects, one layer
// chain, oracled by g++ AND clang++ -O0 on the identical twin (ab/abx/abb):
//
// 1. A parens direct-init declarator in a for-init (`for (string s("ab");`)
//    is a DECLARATION whose trailing ';' belongs to the STATEMENT layer.
//    parseDeclaration's class ctor-call arm used to consume the ';' itself —
//    the only declaration flow that did — so TokenFOR::parse's init tail
//    threw "Expecting ';' after for init" at the CONDITION token, for every
//    class type.
// 2. A loop-header expression that materializes a temporary (the for
//    increment's `s + "x"`, the while condition's `t + "!"`) must construct
//    it EVERY iteration inside the loop's own scope. The temp's decl+init
//    used to flush ONCE into the enclosing block — evaluated once (the while
//    would spin forever on the stale value; the suite timeout would catch a
//    regression) and, for a class-shape for-init, emitted BEFORE the loop
//    variable it reads existed ("undeclared identifier s"). Lowered now as a
//    statement-expression per header (loop_header_expr_scope).
#include <cstdio>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main()
{
	for ( std::string s("ab"); s.size() < 4; s = s + "x" )
		std::cout << s << std::endl;
	std::string t("a");
	while ( (t + "!").size() < 4 )
		t = t + "b";
	std::cout << t << std::endl;
	return 0;
}
